ACTUAL SOLUTION REQUIRED:

Per TERMINAL_ARCHITECTURE_ISSUE.md, three options exist:

OPTION A: Make FrameworkShellManager Async-Native (Recommended)
  - Rewrite all methods to use asyncio.subprocess instead of subprocess
  - Replace threading.RLock with asyncio.Lock
  - Use aiofiles for all file I/O operations
  - Replace os.kill/os.waitpid with async equivalents
  - Major refactor affecting entire framework
  - BENEFIT: Proper ASGI integration, no thread pool overhead
  - DRAWBACK: Large code change, affects all apps using framework shells

OPTION B: Separate Process for Shell Manager
  - Run FrameworkShellManager in dedicated process
  - Communicate via IPC (ZMQ, pipes, or HTTP)
  - ASGI workers send async requests to manager process
  - Manager handles all blocking I/O in isolation
  - BENEFIT: No code changes to FrameworkShellManager internals
  - DRAWBACK: IPC overhead, more complex deployment

OPTION C: Dedicated Thread Pool with Enhanced Locking
  - Keep FrameworkShellManager synchronous
  - Run in dedicated thread pool executor
  - Replace threading.RLock with more sophisticated locking
  - BENEFIT: Minimal code changes
  - DRAWBACK: Thread pool overhead, may still have edge cases
  - NOTE: Logs indicate this approach already failed

RECOMMENDED APPROACH: Option A (Async-Native Refactor)

Because:
  1. Option C was already attempted and failed (per investigation logs)
  2. ASGI architecture fundamentally incompatible with blocking I/O
  3. Long-term maintainability - no sync/async bridge complexity
  4. Better performance - no thread pool context switching
  5. Sets pattern for other framework components

